IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `oapa-nouns`

-- Offences against the Person Act 1861 -- LAYER 1: CHILD-STEALING (section 56, blank
-- in this text) AND BIGAMY (section 57).
--
-- Section 57 is the one place in this text where a list of exceptions (a proviso with
-- four limbs) takes people out of an offence, so each exception is its own fact.

§ `Child-stealing and Bigamy`

§§ `Section 56: child-stealing (text blank)`

-- src:90 | 56 Child-stealing.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 56 (src:90)
GIVETH A STRING
`s 56: only the title survives in this text` MEANS
    "Section 56 keeps its title, Child-stealing, but its text is a row of dots; nothing is encoded."

§§ `Section 57: bigamy`

-- src:93 | 57 Bigamy. Offence may be dealt with where offender shall be apprehended. Not to extend to second marriages, &c. herein stated.
-- src:94 | Whosoever, being married, shall marry any other person during the life of the former husband or wife, whether the second marriage shall have taken place in England or Ireland or elsewhere, shall be guilty of felony, and being convicted thereof shall be liable . . . to be kept in penal servitude for any term not exceeding seven years . . . :
-- src:95 | Provided, that nothing in this section contained shall extend to any second marriage contracted elsewhere than in England and Ireland by any other than a subject of Her Majesty, or to any person marrying a second time whose husband or wife shall have been continually absent from such person for the space of seven years then last past, and shall not have been known by such person to be living within that time, or shall extend to any person who, at the time of such second marriage, shall have been divorced from the bond of the first marriage, or to any person whose former marriage shall have been declared void by the sentence of any court of competent jurisdiction.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 57 (src:93-95)
-- The offence: a person "being married" who "shall marry any other person during the
-- life of the former husband or wife, whether the second marriage shall have taken
-- place in England or Ireland or elsewhere".
-- Four exceptions in the proviso, any one of which takes the person out:
--  (1) "any second marriage contracted elsewhere than in England and Ireland by any
--      other than a subject of Her Majesty": outside England and Ireland AND not a
--      subject;
--  (2) "any person marrying a second time whose husband or wife shall have been
--      continually absent from such person for the space of seven years then last
--      past, and shall not have been known by such person to be living within that
--      time": seven complete years of continual absence just before the second
--      marriage, AND the person did not know the spouse to be living in that time;
--  (3) "any person who, at the time of such second marriage, shall have been
--      divorced from the bond of the first marriage";
--  (4) "any person whose former marriage shall have been declared void by the
--      sentence of any court of competent jurisdiction".
-- The text does not say whether a declaration of voidness made AFTER the second
-- marriage counts; the encoding reads the fact as "had been declared void" at the time
-- of the second marriage (see NOTES.md).
DECLARE `Conduct under section 57` HAS
    `the person was married` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person married another person` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the former husband or wife was then living` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the second marriage took place in England or Ireland` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person is a subject of Her Majesty` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the number of complete years the husband or wife had been continually absent` IS A NUMBER
    `the person had known the husband or wife to be living within that time` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person had been divorced from the bond of the first marriage` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the former marriage had been declared void by the sentence of a court of competent jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN

-- Exception (1).
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 57 (src:95)
GIVEN c IS A `Conduct under section 57`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 57: exception for a second marriage contracted abroad by a person who is not a subject` c MEANS
        NOT (c's `the second marriage took place in England or Ireland`)
    AND NOT (c's `the person is a subject of Her Majesty`)

-- Exception (2).
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 57 (src:95)
GIVEN c IS A `Conduct under section 57`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 57: exception for a spouse continually absent seven years and not known to be living` c MEANS
        c's `the number of complete years the husband or wife had been continually absent` AT LEAST 7
    AND NOT (c's `the person had known the husband or wife to be living within that time`)

-- Exceptions (3) and (4).
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 57 (src:95)
GIVEN c IS A `Conduct under section 57`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 57: exception for a person divorced from the first marriage` c MEANS
    c's `the person had been divorced from the bond of the first marriage`

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 57 (src:95)
GIVEN c IS A `Conduct under section 57`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 57: exception for a former marriage declared void` c MEANS
    c's `the former marriage had been declared void by the sentence of a court of competent jurisdiction`

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 57 (src:94-95)
GIVEN c IS A `Conduct under section 57`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 57: bigamy` c MEANS
        c's `the person was married`
    AND c's `the person married another person`
    AND c's `the former husband or wife was then living`
    AND NOT (`s 57: exception for a second marriage contracted abroad by a person who is not a subject` c)
    AND NOT (`s 57: exception for a spouse continually absent seven years and not known to be living` c)
    AND NOT (`s 57: exception for a person divorced from the first marriage` c)
    AND NOT (`s 57: exception for a former marriage declared void` c)

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 57 (src:94)
GIVETH A Classification
`s 57: what the text calls the offence` MEANS felony

-- "penal servitude for any term not exceeding seven years": 84 months.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 57 (src:94)
GIVETH A LIST OF Sentence
`s 57: the sentences the text states` MEANS
    LIST `penal servitude` (`for a term not exceeding` 84)
